# Copy to /etc/timberfs/limits.conf.
#
# What this machine will let ONE REQUEST ask for. Each key is optional and
# unset by default, which is no ceiling at all.
#
# They bound a timberfs-query-document(5) and not the flags beside it: a
# document is a request from somewhere else, where the flags are the
# operator at a shell.
#
# A `max` or `deadline` over a ceiling is LOWERED, and the answer is then
# the first page of the one that was asked for — stream-end says which
# ceiling stopped it and the position records resume it. A `tail` over one
# is refused, because a tail answer carries no position to continue from.
#
# The ceilings are declared in every answer that has somewhere to put them,
# so a caller sizes its pages before it asks rather than after an answer
# came back short.
#
# ⚠ This bounds accidents, not adversaries: whoever controls the argv or
# the environment controls these too.

# Most entries one answer may deliver.
#MAX_ENTRIES=100000

# The same in chunks — the unit a `chunks` answer is bounded in, since an
# entry count there caps entries nobody asked about.
#MAX_CHUNKS=5000

# Longest one search may run. It bounds what the other two cannot: a read
# is slow because it READS a lot, not because it matches a lot.
#DEADLINE_MS=30000
